} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[5]{-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" 13194139534544 true] [17592186293978 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293979 13194139534544 true] [17592186293978 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534544 true] [17592186293978 1041 "field-1" 13194139534544 true] [17592186293978 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293979 13194139534544 true] [17592186293978 1110 #uuid "ca32e62c-fcb6-51a7-bc44-8fcea252735a" 13194139534544 true] [17592186293979 1034 "0" 13194139534544 true] [17592186293979 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534544 true] [17592186293979 1111 #uuid "c8dcc751-8cfc-5b4f-ae52-262b527d6cb1" 13194139534544 true] [17592186293980 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293981 13194139534544 true] [17592186293980 1089 "q-88692" 13194139534544 true] [17592186293980 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534544 true] [17592186293980 1100 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139534544 true] [17592186293980 1113 #uuid "72674b4f-6b99-5086-b3af-6306160b6663" 13194139534544 true] [17592186293980 1145 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(-x)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4x)